###1 Part 1: In-Class

df = read.csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/franvillamil/AQM2/refs/heads/master/datasets/other/minwage.csv")

##1.1 Data and its exploration

#a)
df = df %>% mutate(NJ = ifelse(location != "PA", 1, 0))
table(df$NJ)
df %>%
  group_by(NJ) %>%
  summarise(
    mean_wage_before = mean(wageBefore, na.rm = TRUE),
    mean_wage_after = mean(wageAfter, na.rm = TRUE))
#Earnings in New Jersey rose from 4.61 to 5.08 following the policy change, whereas earnings in Pennsylvania remained relatively stable, even slightly declining from 4.65 to 4.61.Earnings in New Jersey rose from 4.61 to 5.08 following the policy change, whereas earnings in Pennsylvania remained relatively stable, even slightly declining from 4.65 to 4.61.

#b)
means = df %>%
  group_by(NJ) %>%
  summarise(
    before = mean(fullBefore, na.rm = TRUE),
    after = mean(fullAfter, na.rm = TRUE),
    change = after - before)
means

nj_change = means$change[means$NJ == 1]
pa_change = means$change[means$NJ == 0]
did_est = nj_change - pa_change
cat("DiD estimate:", round(did_est, 3), "\n")

#The DiD explains how NJ and PA differ from one another. Employment in PA evolved more favorably when the DiD is positive, as it is with DID, 2.927.

#c)
df_long = df %>%
  mutate(id = row_number()) %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = c(fullBefore, fullAfter),
    names_to = "period",
    values_to = "full_emp") %>%
  mutate(
    post = ifelse(period == "fullAfter", 1, 0),
    NJ = ifelse(location != "PA", 1, 0))
nrow(df_long)
nrow(df)
#Because the DiD estimate is the interaction post NJ, which captures the differences between the within-NJ change in employment (post-pre) and the equivalent within-PA change, the DiD regression needs a lengthy structure.

##1.2 Difference in Diferrence regression

#a)
m_did = feols(full_emp ~ post * NJ, data = df_long, cluster = ~id)
modelsummary(m_did, stars = TRUE, gof_map = c("nobs", "r.squared"),
             output = "html")
#As in 1.1b, the interaction term post x NJ's coefficient is 2.927.

#b)
m_did_fe = feols(full_emp ~ post * NJ | chain, data = df_long, cluster = ~id)
modelsummary(
  list("DiD" = m_did, "DiD + Chain FE" = m_did_fe),
  stars = TRUE, gof_map = c("nobs", "r.squared"),
  output = "html")

#The DiD estimation remains unchanged when chain type is taken into account. 
#Although chain FEs absorb baseline variations in staffing levels among fast-food chains, adjusting for chain type has minimal effect on the DiD coefficient since chain types are approximately balanced across states.

#c)
#The parallel trends assumption means that, if the minimum wage had not increased in New Jersey, employment in fast-food restaurants in New Jersey and Pennsylvania would have followed similar patterns between February and November 1992. 
#This assumption seems reasonable because the two states operate in comparable economic contexts, and the short time gap between surveys reduces the likelihood of different underlying trends. 
#However this assumption would be violated if New Jersey were affected by a separate economic shock during this period that did not also impact Pennsylvania.

##1.3 Wages as a validation check

#a)
df_long_wage = df %>%
  mutate(id = row_number()) %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = c(wageBefore, wageAfter),
    names_to = "period",
    values_to = "wage") %>%
  mutate(
    post = ifelse(period == "wageAfter", 1, 0),
    NJ = ifelse(location != "PA", 1, 0))
m_wage = feols(wage ~ post * NJ, data = df_long_wage, cluster = ~id)
summary(m_wage)

#The interaction term post:NJ has a coefficient of 0.509, indicating that wages increased significantly more in New Jersey than in Pennsylvania following the treatment period.

#b)
# The wage DiD serves as a first-stage or validity check.
# If wages had not increased in New Jersey following the minimum wage change,it would be unclear whether the treatment was actually implemented.
# The observed increase in wages in New Jersey confirms that the policy took effect.
# This strengthens the credibility of the employment DiD, allowing it to be interpreted as a causal effect of the minimum wage increase rather than a coincidental comparison.

