##2.2 Naive TWFE and Callaway-Estimator

#a)
mpdta = mpdta %>%
  mutate(treated_post = as.integer(first.treat > 0 & year >= first.treat))
m_twfe = feols(lemp ~ treated_post | countyreal + year,
               data = mpdta, cluster = ~countyreal)
summary(m_twfe)

#The treated_post coefficient is -0.0365. 
#This indicates that the therapy is linked to a 3.65% drop in teen employment.

#b)
cs_out = att_gt(
  yname = "lemp",
  gname = "first.treat",
  idname = "countyreal",
  tname = "year",
  xformla = ~ lpop,
  data = mpdta,
  control_group = "nevertreated")
# Aggregate to an overall ATT
aggte(cs_out, type = "simple")

#The total ATT estimate is -0.0418. This implies that the therapy lowers teen employment by an average of around 4.18%.

#c)
cs_dyn <- aggte(cs_out, type = "dynamic")
p_dyn <- ggdid(cs_dyn)
p_dyn

ggsave(
  filename = "event_study_cs.png",
  plot = p_dyn,
  width = 8,
  height = 5,
  dpi = 300
)

#There are no statistically significant variations in trends before to treatment, as indicated by the fact that all of the pre-treatment estimates' confidence ranges contain zero. As a result, the parallel trends hypothesis seems reasonable. 
#There is a distinct downward trend in the post-treatment estimations. 
#Over time, the estimates become increasingly negative, even if the immediate effect at t = 0 is tiny and not statistically significant.
#Showing that the therapy has a detrimental and increasing effect on teen employment over time.

##2.3 Pre-testing trends assumption

#a)
cs_out_bt = att_gt(
  yname = "lemp",
  gname = "first.treat",
  idname = "countyreal",
  tname = "year",
  xformla = ~ lpop,
  data = mpdta,
  control_group = "nevertreated",
  bstrap = TRUE,
  cband = TRUE)
summary(cs_out_bt)

#The p-value is 0.233.
#The null hypothesis and all pre-treatment effects are zero.
#Since the p-value is big, failing to reject the null hypothesis.

#b)
ggdid(cs_out_bt)
ggsave("mpdta_att_gt.pdf", width = 10, height = 6)

#All pre-treatment ATT(g,t) estimates include 0 in their confidence interval

#c)
#If the pre-test is not rejected, it simply means that there is no proof of differences during the pre-treatment phase. 
#Violations that follow treatment or differences that are not statistically observed cannot be ruled out by the test. 
#As a result, it offers no solid proof of the parallel trends hypothesis.

##2.4 Comparing the control groups features

#a)
cs_out_nyt = att_gt(
  yname = "lemp",
  gname = "first.treat",
  idname = "countyreal",
  tname = "year",
  xformla = ~ lpop,
  data = mpdta,
  control_group = "notyettreated")
aggte(cs_out_nyt, type = "simple")

#With untreated counties, the ATT is -0.0414. In contrast, the ATT for counties that were never treated was -0.0418. 
#The sign and size of the two estimations are nearly the same.

#b)
cs_dyn_nyt = aggte(cs_out_nyt, type = "dynamic")
ggdid(cs_dyn_nyt)
ggsave("mpdta_event_study_nyt.pdf", width = 7, height = 4)

#The outcomes are comparable to those in section 2.2c; the general conclusion is that the therapy lowers teen employment, and the impact increases with time without changing.

#c)
#When anticipatory effects are an issue, it is better to use the control group, which was never treated. 
#By increasing the number of control observations, the untreated control group can increase accuracy, however this is predicated on the idea that present results are unaffected by future treatment. 
#When anticipatory effects are doubtful, it is better to use untreated controls.

##2.5 Discussion: TWFE fails in staggered settings?

#a)
#To estimate the treatment impact, a TWFE incorporates both unit and time fixed effects. 
#The "forbidden comparison" issue arises in staggered DiD settings when the TWFE model uses already-treated units for later-treated units as a control; never-treated units, not yet treated units, and already-treated units are used as a control group. 
#This can lead to misleading results because treatment effect can be heterogeneous over time and units. 
#Because the control is impacted by the treatment, this may present issues if the treatment effect varies over time and among cohorts.

#b)
#The TWFE's estimator is -0.0365, whereas the Callaway-Santanna estimate is -0.0418. 
#These differences are negligible and point in the same direction.
